<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nagasena</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 2nd century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nagasena was a Buddhist monk who, according to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milindapanha">Milindapanha</a>, debated questions of doctrine with King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menander-i">Milinda</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek</a> ruler Menander I, and won him over to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>. His arguments, notably the image of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> to explain that there is no permanent self, became classic statements of Buddhist teaching. Little is known of him beyond this text.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nagasena.jpg" alt="Nagasena" width="320" height="237" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Horace van Ruith · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Milinda_ask_questions.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
